## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem validates DP mastery (two implementations) and handling of substring lookups with a dictionary. It stresses practical constraints and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bottom-up DP, Memoization, Time: O(N * maxLen)
- Hash-map lookups for dictionary scores
- Edge cases: no decomposition, empty input, overlapping choices

## Code Example
```javascript
// Bottom-up
function maxScore(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const n = s.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(NEG);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(i, maxLen); len++) {
      const substr = s.slice(i - len, i);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr) && dp[i - len] !== NEG) {
        dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[i - len] + dict[substr]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === NEG ? -1 : dp[n];
}

// Top-down
function maxScoreTopDown(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const memo = new Map();
  function solve(i) {
    if (i === s.length) return 0;
    if (memo.has(i)) return memo.get(i);
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(maxLen, s.length - i); len++) {
      const substr = s.substring(i, i + len);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr)) {
        const val = dict[substr] + solve(i + len);
        if (val > best) best = val;
      }
    }
    memo.set(i, best);
    return best;
  }
  const res = solve(0);
  return res === -Infinity ? -1 : res;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt it if dictionary lookups are expensive and you need pruning?
- How would you extend to handle negative scores or required minimum total?

