## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic dictionary updates with a prefix-based query, combining trie augmentation and edge-case handling. 

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node passCount to track subtree content
- O(length of word) updates for insert/delete
- LCP query via prefix traversal until no valid child
- Edge cases: duplicates, deleting absent words, memory pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); this.hasWord=new Set(); }
  insert(w){ if(this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.add(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.cnt++; } }
  delete(w){ if(!this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.delete(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const child=node.next.get(ch); child.cnt--; if(child.cnt===0){ node.next.delete(ch); } node=child; } }
  lcpQuery(q){ let node=this.root, depth=0, res=0; for(const ch of q){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) break; node=node.next.get(ch); depth++; if(node.cnt>0) res=depth; } return res; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support Unicode or a larger alphabet efficiently? 
- Compare memory trade-offs with an augmented BST of words versus the Trie approach.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to adapt sorting techniques to handle duplicate elements efficiently and to analyze complexity trade-offs between quicksort variants and mergesort.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partition using Dutch national flag algorithm
- Average vs worst-case complexity with duplicate keys
- In-place sorting with tail recursion optimization
- Space complexity comparison between quicksort and mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi) with lt/gt pointers
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  const pivot = a[lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;

  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) swap(a, lt++, i++);
    else if (a[i] > pivot) swap(a, i, gt--);
    else i++;
  }

  // Tail recurse on smaller subarray for O(log n) space
  if (gt - lo < hi - lt) {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt + 1;
  } else {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
    hi = lt - 1;
  }

  if (lo < hi) threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi);
}
```

## Test Plan
- **All equal**: Verify no swaps and linear time
- **Already sorted**: Confirm correct partitioning and maintain sorted order
- **No duplicates**: Validate against standard quicksort behavior
- **Random with duplicates**: Compare against built-in sort for correctness
- **Large duplicate ratio**: Measure performance improvement vs standard quicksort

## Complexity Comparison
- **Three-way quicksort**: O(n log m) average, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space
- **Standard mergesort**: O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space

Optimal when m ≪ n (many duplicate keys).

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a standard data structure with array-based tree, index math, and clear complexity expectations.

## Key Concepts
- Segment tree
- Range maximum
- Point updates
- Space/time complexity

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(arr) {
    this.n = arr.length;
    this.size = 1;
    while (this.size < this.n) this.size <<= 1;
    this.tree = new Array(this.size * 2).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i < this.n; i++) this.tree[this.size + i] = arr[i];
    for (let i = this.size - 1; i > 0; i--) this.tree[i] = Math.max(this.tree[i<<1], this.tree[i<<1|1]);
  }
  update(idx, val) {
    let pos = this.size + idx;
    this.tree[pos] = val;
    for (pos >>= 1; pos > 0; pos >>= 1) {
      this.tree[pos] = Math.max(this.tree[pos<<1], this.tree[pos<<1|1]);
    }
  }
  query(l, r) {
    let res = -Infinity;
    l += this.size; r += this.size;
    while (l <= r) {
      if (l & 1) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[l++]);
      if (!(r & 1)) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[r--]);
      l >>= 1; r >>= 1;
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

